<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">npm</span><span style="color: rgb(206, 145, 120);"> -v</span></span></code></pre></div><div></div></div><div class="false flex flex-col gap-2 h-full"></div></div></div>Both should show a version number (e.g. <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-300">v20.x.x</span> and <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-300">10.x.x</span>).
If either says “not recognized”, install Node.js from <a href="https://nodejs.org/" node="[object Object]" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://nodejs.org</a> (choose the <strong class="font-semibold">LTS</strong> version), then <strong class="font-semibold">close and reopen VS Code</strong>, and try again.</li>
<li class="break-words whitespace-pre-wrap [&amp;&gt;ul]:whitespace-normal [&amp;&gt;ol]:whitespace-normal">Make sure the terminal is inside the folder that contains <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-300">package.json</span>
(your path already looks correct: <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-300">...\Tesla-Energy-main\Tesla-Energy-main</span>).</li>
</ol>
<hr class="border-border-l2 clear-both">
